---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/ifrspublications/preface.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Purpose of the Preface" },
        { id: "s2", label: "IFRS Foundation Structure" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The IASB – Role & Composition" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Objectives of the Foundation and IASB" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Scope & Authority of IFRS Standards" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Due Process" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Interpretations – the IFRS Interpretations Committee" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Who Is Expected to Apply IFRS Standards" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Effective Date & Transition" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Language & Translation" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Relationship with Other Pronouncements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "History – IASC to IASB" },
        { id: "s13", label: "IASB vs FASB Standard-Setting Structure" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Purpose of the Preface</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">An orientation document, not a technical standard</div>
        <div class="principle">The Preface explains the OBJECTIVES and DUE PROCESS of the IFRS Foundation and the International Accounting Standards Board (IASB), and explains the SCOPE, AUTHORITY, and APPLICABILITY of the documents that form part of "IFRS Standards" – it contains no technical accounting requirements itself, but frames how every other pronouncement in the library should be read and understood.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>IFRS Foundation Structure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">A three-tier governance structure</div>
        <div class="principle">The IFRS Foundation is an independent, not-for-profit organisation. Its MONITORING BOARD provides a formal link between the Trustees and public authorities (such as securities regulators). The TRUSTEES are responsible for governance and oversight, including appointing IASB members and securing funding, but have NO involvement in technical, standard-setting matters.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Supporting bodies</div>
        <div class="principle">The Foundation also oversees the IFRS INTERPRETATIONS COMMITTEE (technical interpretations), the IFRS ADVISORY COUNCIL (strategic advice from a broad range of stakeholders), and – since 2021 – the INTERNATIONAL SUSTAINABILITY STANDARDS BOARD (ISSB), which develops IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards alongside the IASB's financial reporting Standards.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The IASB – Role & Composition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The standard-setting body itself</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The IASB is the INDEPENDENT standard-setting body of the IFRS Foundation, responsible for developing and issuing IFRS Standards, including the IFRS for SMEs Standard, and for approving Interpretations developed by the IFRS Interpretations Committee. Members are appointed by the Trustees based on professional competence and practical experience, with the Trustees also required to ensure an appropriate balance of professional backgrounds and geographical diversity among IASB members.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Objectives of the Foundation and IASB</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What the Foundation exists to achieve</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Develop, in the public interest, a single set of high-quality, understandable, enforceable, and globally accepted IFRS Standards based on clearly articulated principles</span>
          <span>Promote the use and rigorous application of those Standards</span>
          <span>Take account of the financial reporting needs of emerging economies and small and medium-sized entities</span>
          <span>Promote and facilitate adoption of IFRS Standards through the convergence of national accounting standards with IFRS Standards</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Scope & Authority of IFRS Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What "IFRS Standards" comprises</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRS Standards are developed with the objective of applying to the GENERAL PURPOSE financial statements of profit-oriented entities – including commercial, industrial, and financial entities, whether organised in corporate or other forms. The term "IFRS Standards" comprises: IFRS Standards issued by the IASB; IAS Standards issued by the predecessor IASC and still in force; and Interpretations (IFRIC and SIC), which are an integral part of the requirements.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Not designed for not-for-profit or public sector entities</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRS Standards are not specifically designed for not-for-profit, government, or public sector entities – though there is no reason preventing such entities applying them, if considered appropriate. Public sector entities more commonly refer to International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSAS), which are separately developed.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Due Process</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">A transparent, consultative process</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-05-25"><span>Stage</span><span>What happens</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-05-25"><span>1</span><span>Agenda consultation and research, drawing on staff research and input from the Advisory Council and other stakeholders</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-05-25"><span>2</span><span>Discussion Paper published for public comment (not always required)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-05-25"><span>3</span><span>Exposure Draft published for public comment – a mandatory step before any new or amended Standard</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-05-25"><span>4</span><span>Redeliberation of comments received, and issuance of the final Standard, requiring the support of at least nine of the fourteen IASB members</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Interpretations – the IFRS Interpretations Committee</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Resolving diversity in practice</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The IFRS Interpretations Committee identifies and addresses questions of application arising in practice where diversity has emerged or seems likely to emerge, or where clarifying guidance is needed on an issue not specifically addressed by existing Standards. Its Interpretations (formerly SIC, now IFRIC) require IASB approval and, once issued, have the SAME AUTHORITY as the Standards themselves.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Who Is Expected to Apply IFRS Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Applicability, not universal mandate</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The IASB itself has no authority to REQUIRE compliance with its Standards – application is determined by individual jurisdictions' legal and regulatory requirements, or by voluntary adoption. IFRS Standards are designed to apply to general purpose financial statements and other financial reporting of all profit-oriented entities; jurisdictions differ in whether they mandate IFRS for listed entities, permit optional adoption, or use IFRS as the basis for local GAAP.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Effective Date & Transition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Standards specify their own transition</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Each Standard specifies its own EFFECTIVE DATE and any transitional provisions, including whether earlier application is permitted. Entities generally apply new or amended Standards RETROSPECTIVELY unless the Standard specifies otherwise (per IAS 8's general transition principles) – many major Standards include specific transition reliefs to ease first-time application of significant changes.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Language & Translation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">English as the authoritative text</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">IFRS Standards are issued in ENGLISH, which is the authoritative text. The IFRS Foundation coordinates TRANSLATION of Standards into other languages through an established translation process, but in the event of any inconsistency between a translation and the English text, the English version prevails.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship with Other Pronouncements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Where the Preface sits relative to the rest of the library</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-18"><span>Pronouncement</span><span>Relationship to the Preface</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-18"><span>Conceptual Framework</span><span>Also not a Standard; describes concepts underlying Standards, whereas the Preface describes the STRUCTURE and PROCESS by which Standards are created</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-18"><span>IFRS Practice Statements</span><span>Non-mandatory guidance documents; entities are not required to comply, unlike Standards and Interpretations</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-18"><span>IFRS for SMEs Standard</span><span>A separate, self-contained Standard developed by the IASB specifically for entities without public accountability</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>History – IASC to IASB</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">A 2001 restructuring</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The IASB's predecessor, the International Accounting Standards Committee (IASC), issued International Accounting Standards (IAS) from 1973 onward. In 2001, the IASC was restructured into the IFRS Foundation and the IASB, which adopted the body of existing IAS Standards and began issuing new Standards under the "IFRS" designation going forward – explaining why the library today contains both "IAS" (older, IASC-era) and "IFRS" (newer, IASB-era) numbered Standards side by side.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>IASB vs FASB Standard-Setting Structure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IASB/IFRS Foundation vs FASB/FAF</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IASB / IFRS Foundation</span><span>FASB / Financial Accounting Foundation (FAF)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Geographic scope</span><span>Global – Standards intended for worldwide adoption across many jurisdictions</span><span>National – standards apply specifically to US GAAP reporting</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Governance layer</span><span>Monitoring Board + Trustees + IASB</span><span>SEC oversight + FAF Board of Trustees + FASB</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Legal authority to mandate use</span><span>None directly – adoption depends on individual jurisdictions</span><span>Similarly, FASB itself has no direct enforcement power, though SEC recognition gives US GAAP de facto mandatory status</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Despite differing geographic scope, both bodies follow broadly similar independent, due-process-driven standard-setting models, and the two boards have periodically pursued formal convergence projects (e.g. on revenue recognition and leases).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Due process", definition: "The IASB's transparent, consultative procedure for developing and amending Standards, involving public comment periods at multiple stages." },
      { term: "Exposure Draft", definition: "A formal, mandatory public consultation document the IASB publishes before finalising any new or significantly amended Standard." },
      { term: "General purpose financial statements", definition: "Financial statements intended to meet the common information needs of a wide range of external users." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.Preface} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>



